-- hyperactive-emits-c (2026-11-21): the paper's §9.4 "Compilation through C" names C as the primary
-- target; what the repository has is lib/x86.cub's builder, which emits x86 bytes into a Linux ELF
-- file and so runs on no other processor. The builder already brings a definition to a first-order
-- form before its templates (Defs: after specialization, then apps, lift, check), and its yes or no
-- (fragment-n, x-ok-n) does not depend on the target, so the C emitter starts from that same form
-- and is one template per construct. Known (2026-11-21): an experiment, sixteen definitions ce-* in
-- lib/x86.cub after jit-plan, covering words, word arithmetic, let and full calls; anything else
-- emits a call that aborts. ce-demo x y = x*7 + y came out as one C function; the system compiler
-- built it; 6 3 gave 45; a result past 64 bits aborts. Printed by `hyperactive view in
-- lib/library.cub ce-demo-c` (lib/x86.cub has no imports and checks only when loaded after others).
-- Nothing in it is proven. To do: data and case, closures, memory, the world's commands, continuing
-- processes; the meaning of the emitted subset of C as a definition of the language (language-support
-- holds C's semantics as its goal), and against it the proof that each template's C means its
-- construct and has no undefined behaviour; then the smallest machine (cells, wires, application,
-- duplicate, erase) written in the language, emitted and run.
hyperactive-emits-c : Node
hyperactive-emits-c = node "Hyperactive emits C for everything the builder accepts, each construct by its template, with the proof that the C means the term and has no undefined behaviour." (cons language-implementation-remainder nil)
